828:     @nogc number
829:     compute_r(const number Shat_by_nuhat, const number nuhat, const number d) const pure {
830:     /*
831:         Notes: (21/06/18)
832:         In a complex numbers calculation, it is possible to get a very small value of
833:         Shat_by_nuhat that intermixes the real and complex components when calculating r.
834: 
835:         This can result in a very large negative r, which is is both unphysical and likely
836:         to overflow when computing fw. The correct behaviour is to set r = 10+0j as Shat
837:         gets small. Finite negative values of Shat should never happen, but even
838:         if they do we still want r = 10+0j, to keep fw in line. This could still malfunction
839:         for very small real values of nuhat, but if anyone tries that they will have larger
840:         problems than just this function.
841: 
842:         @author: Nick Gibbons
843:     */
844:         number l = nuhat*nuhat/kappa/kappa/d/d;
845:         number r;
846: 
847:         if (Shat_by_nuhat*rlimit<=l) {
848:             r = rlimit;
849:         } else {
850:             r = l/Shat_by_nuhat;
851:         }
852:         return r;
853:     }
